// GENERATED BY `yarn ingest` - DO NOT EDIT BY HAND.
// Built 2026-11-18T18:12:21.409Z from src/data/projects.js + src/data/profile.js.
export default {"version":1,"builtAt":"2026-11-18T18:12:21.409Z","embedModel":null,"embedDim":null,"chunks":[{"id":"phishingnet:authored:0","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"authored","heading":"PhishingNet","text":"PhishingNet - Explainable phishing-email analysis — every score traces back to the words that caused it.\nAn email analyzer that explains itself. Paste in an email and it returns a\nphishing/suspicious/legitimate verdict plus 19 separately scored signals — 12 manipulation\ntactics such as urgency, fear, reward bait, authority impersonation and credential harvesting,\nand 7 checks on the raw message such as sender authentication, link deception and attachment\nrisk — each with a plain-English explanation and the exact text that triggered it. The signals\nare fused with a TF-IDF/logistic-regression classifier through a transparent noisy-OR, and a\nheader-aware trust step lowers the risk for authenticated mail from established senders, so\ngenuine security alerts and receipts aren't flagged. It ships as a\npublished Chrome extension that scores emails inline in Gmail and Outlook, plus a CLI, a\nlocal FastAPI server and an Electron/React desktop app — all over one Python engine, and\nall running locally, so no email ever leaves the machine.\nBuilt with: Python, scikit-learn, FastAPI, Chrome Extension, Electron, React.\nAlso referred to as: TIS, phishing analyzer, phishing detector, email analyzer, Grammarly for phishing, phishing net, chrome extension, gmail, outlook.\nRole: Solo.\nYear: 2026. Status: live.\nInterpretability first: every attribute score comes back with character-offset evidence spans, and the classifier reports the exact coefficient x tf-idf contribution of each keyword that moved its prediction.\nScores 0.9912 F1 (classifier alone) and 0.9820 F1 (combined tool) on a 10,122-email held-out set drawn from six public corpora.\nThe evaluation deliberately surfaces its own weakness: the legitimate baseline is 2000s Enron corporate mail, so the model partly learns \"corporate style = safe\". That leakage is measured per-source and documented rather than hidden.\nAttribute scorers are independent, testable modules, and the lexicons were validated by frequency-lift against real phishing samples instead of being guessed.\nPublished on the Chrome Web Store: it reads each email you open in Gmail or Outlook and shows an inline verdict together with the reasons behind it.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:authored:1","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"authored","heading":"PhishingNet > notes","text":"PhishingNet has its own project page on this site at /phishingnet, with screenshots, results, a dev log and the full research paper as a PDF. The research paper was written in July 2026 with mentor Siddarth Mohapatra. The problem: built-in filters in Gmail and Outlook are black boxes that block or allow email without saying why, so people never learn the warning signs, and heavy workload makes people more vulnerable to phishing. Data: 67,472 emails from six public datasets (Enron, CEAS-08, Ling-Spam, Nazario, a Nigerian-fraud set, SpamAssassin), split into 47,230 training, 10,120 validation and 10,122 test emails. Results on the 10,122 unseen test emails: accuracy 0.9925, precision 0.9916, recall 0.9907, F1 0.9912, ROC-AUC 0.9995. Retraining with genuine transactional email cut false positives on that kind of mail from 79% to 0%. Design decisions: TF-IDF with logistic regression was chosen over CNNs, BERT and LLM APIs because it is accurate, fast, small and interpretable, and an LLM API would send private email to a third party. Noisy-OR was chosen over averaging so one strong signal can raise the risk on its own. Verdict bands: below 0.35 legitimate, 0.6 and above phishing, otherwise suspicious. A browser extension was chosen over a website or a Gmail add-on. Ethics and privacy: trained only on public anonymised datasets; the extension checks only the open email, analyses it in memory and does not store it; it is advisory and never deletes or reports email.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:authored:2","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"authored","heading":"PhishingNet > notes","text":"Current research and testing next: Devesh is running an online study asking whether explaining why an email is suspicious helps people more than a simple phishing or not phishing label. Participants sort 30 realistic but fake work emails on their own device from a link, in three parts: no help as a baseline; then with the tool, where half see only the verdict and half also see the reasons with warning signs highlighted; then no help again to test whether they learned. It measures accuracy, risky actions such as clicking a scam link, false alarms and confidence, with no real emails and no personal data. The study is built and launching after ethics review. Future work: brand-impersonation detection and Outlook support are done; still planned are feeding link destination domain age into the score, catching phishing in images, QR codes and attachments, supporting other languages and retraining on new tactics.","vector":null},{"id":"portfolio-site:authored:0","projectId":"portfolio-site","title":"Portfolio Site","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/portfolio","sourceType":"authored","heading":"Portfolio Site","text":"Portfolio Site - This site — a React SPA with a RAG chatbot that answers questions about my work.\nA personal portfolio built with React and Vite and deployed on Vercel.\nIt includes a retrieval-augmented chatbot that answers questions about my projects,\ngrounded in my own project notes and the READMEs of the repos behind them. Retrieval is\nhybrid — keyword scoring plus embeddings — and the index is built ahead of time by a\nscript rather than scraped live, so answers are fast and the bot can cite its sources.\nBuilt with: React, Vite, React Router, Vercel Functions, RAG.\nAlso referred to as: portfolio, this site, the chatbot, personal website, rag bot.\nRole: Solo.\nYear: 2026. Status: wip.\nHybrid retrieval: BM25 keyword scoring fused with embeddings, so a rate-limited embedding API degrades quality instead of breaking the bot.\nThe projects data file is the single source of truth — it renders the Work page, builds the retrieval index, and generates the prompt catalog.\nThe full project roster is injected into every prompt, which is what lets the bot answer \"what have you built?\" — a question top-k retrieval fails at by construction.","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:authored:0","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"authored","heading":"AgentDock","text":"AgentDock - A task-delegation bulletin board where AI agents hand work to each other.\nA shared, stateless place for AI agents to delegate asynchronously. Any agent\ncan post a task it needs help with; others discover open tasks, claim one, and post a result\nback. Node and Express with SQLite through Node's built-in module, so installing it pulls\nExpress and nothing else. Agent-facing documentation is served live from the API itself,\nand a read-only browser dashboard shows tasks moving from open to claimed to done.\nBuilt with: Node.js, Express, SQLite.\nAlso referred to as: NANDA, agent dock, agent tasks, task board, agent delegation, bulletin board.\nRole: Solo.\nYear: 2026. Status: archived.\nUses Node's built-in node:sqlite rather than better-sqlite3 specifically to avoid native compilation, so npm install cannot fail on an unfamiliar machine or host.\nServes its own agent-facing skill documentation from a live endpoint, so an agent can discover how to use the API without out-of-band docs.\nDeliberately unauthenticated as an MVP — the friction of auth would have defeated the point of a drop-in coordination surface.","vector":null},{"id":"lyricmatcher:authored:0","projectId":"lyricmatcher","title":"Resonate","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/lyricmatcher","sourceType":"authored","heading":"Resonate","text":"Resonate - Recommends songs by what the lyrics actually say, not by genre or listening history.\nA recommender that works from lyrical content rather than collaborative\nfiltering. Name a song and artist and it returns five tracks whose lyrics carry a similar\nemotional register. Matching blends three signals — word-level TF-IDF, character-level\nTF-IDF, and MiniLM sentence embeddings — weighted 30/20/50, with a small same-artist bonus\nand a penalty that stops identical titles dominating the results. Songs are looked up by\nfuzzy match, and anything missing from the dataset is fetched from Genius on demand and\nfolded into the index. A FastAPI backend serves a Vite/TypeScript/Tailwind frontend, which\nalso resolves YouTube links and cover art for each recommendation.\nBuilt with: Python, FastAPI, sentence-transformers, scikit-learn, TypeScript, React.\nAlso referred to as: lyricmatcher, lyric matcher, recommendr, music recommender, song recommender, lyrics, emotion music.\nRole: Solo.\nYear: 2026. Status: live.\nEmbeddings and TF-IDF matrices are cached to disk and keyed by a dataset signature hash, with an incremental path that re-encodes only appended rows instead of the whole corpus.\nSongs missing from the dataset are fetched from the Genius API at query time, appended, and re-indexed — so an unknown song is a cache miss rather than a failure.\nFuzzy song lookup means a near-miss on spelling or punctuation still finds the track.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:bio","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer","sourceType":"authored","heading":"About","text":"Devesh Gupta - Student developer and researcher working on phishing defence\nI am a student developer and researcher who builds web applications and machine learning tools. My main project is PhishingNet, an explainable phishing detector, and I am now running a study on whether explaining why an email is suspicious helps people more than a simple warning. I am also developing an interest in criminology and want to do more projects in that field after PhishingNet.\nLocated in Dubai, UAE.\nOpen to collaborating on projects and to mentorship in security and criminology research.\nlanguages: Python, JavaScript, TypeScript. frameworks: React, FastAPI, Express, Vite, Electron. libraries: scikit-learn, sentence-transformers. tools: Git, SQLite, Vercel, Node.js, Chrome Extension\nContact: guptadevesh@hotmail.com","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:exp0","projectId":null,"title":"Work Experience Placement at ALPTech","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"Experience","text":"Work Experience Placement at ALPTech (2026-02 - 2026-02)\nA two-week placement where I learned how options trading works and built a Brownian motion model to price options.\nBuilt a Brownian motion simulation to predict option prices, and adapted it for options markets in different regions.\nExtended the model with extra parameters, including variance and volatility of volatility.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:edu0","projectId":null,"title":"GCSEs, then A-Levels, JESS, Dubai","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"Education","text":"Devesh Gupta is a student at JESS, Dubai.\nHe is studying GCSEs, then A-Levels at JESS, Dubai (2022-09 - expected 2029-06).\nSchool / college / university: JESS, Dubai. Qualification or degree: GCSEs, then A-Levels.\nHe has not graduated yet; expected graduation 2029-06.\nThe school is located in Dubai, UAE.\nSelected for The Innovation Story Fellowship, 2026.\nCompleted the AWS Bootcamp, 2025.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq0","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: How do I get in touch?\nA: Email is best: guptadevesh@hotmail.com. Code is at github.com/dubaiplayer.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq1","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: What is your main project?\nA: PhishingNet is a lightweight machine learning model that performs full semantic analysis of fraudulent emails, scoring the top manipulation tactics and other indicators. It is my main project and I am still improving it.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq2","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: Why did you build PhishingNet?\nA: A family member was caught out by a phishing email. Most tools just block or flag messages without explaining why, so people never learn to spot the warning signs themselves. I wanted a tool that shows its reasons.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq3","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: What research are you doing with PhishingNet?\nA: I am running a study on whether explaining why an email is suspicious helps people more than a simple \"phishing / not phishing\" label, and whether they get better at spotting phishing on their own afterwards.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq4","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: What are you looking for?\nA: I am open to collaborating on projects and to mentorship in security and criminology research.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq5","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: What is your strongest programming language?\nA: Python. PhishingNet and Resonate are both built on Python, using scikit-learn, FastAPI and sentence-transformers.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq6","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: What are you learning right now?\nA: R and data visualisation, so I can analyse and present the results of my phishing study.","vector":null},{"id":"profile:authored:faq7","projectId":null,"title":"Devesh Gupta","sourceUrl":null,"sourceType":"authored","heading":"FAQ","text":"Q: Do you have work experience?\nA: Yes. In February 2026 I did a two-week placement at ALPTech, where I learned about options trading and built a Brownian motion model to price options, including volatility of volatility.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:0","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet","text":"PhishingNet\n\nLanguages: Python, JavaScript, HTML, CSS\n\n**\"Grammarly for phishing.\"** Paste or open an email and get an explainable,\nstructured breakdown of its manipulation / phishing tactics — each attribute\nscored, with the exact triggering evidence highlighted — plus one overall risk\nverdict. Interpretability first: every score traces back to evidence, not a black\nbox, and everything runs **100% locally** (no email leaves your machine).\n\n---","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:1","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > What it is","text":"PhishingNet > What it is\n\nTIS reads a raw email (headers + body, or just body) and returns:\n\n- **An overall risk verdict** — `phishing` / `suspicious` / `legitimate` with a 0–1 score.\n- **12 per-attribute scores** — urgency, fear/threat, reward bait, authority\n  impersonation, financial requests, credential harvesting, generic greetings,\n  sender-domain spoofing, suspicious links, grammar, caps/tone — each with a\n  plain-English explanation and **character-offset evidence spans** for highlighting.\n- **Classifier keyword attributions** — the specific words/n-grams that drove the\n  ML model's prediction (exact `coef × tf-idf` contributions), with intensities.\n\nIt ships three front-ends over one Python engine: a **CLI**, a local **FastAPI\nserver**, and a **desktop app** (Electron + React).","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:2","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Key features","text":"PhishingNet > Key features\n\n- 🔍 **Explainable** — per-attribute evidence spans + per-keyword ML attributions.\n- 🧠 **Hybrid detection** — 10 rule/lexicon attributes + 2 heuristics + a TF-IDF/\n  Logistic-Regression classifier, fused by a transparent, tunable **noisy-OR**.\n- 🔒 **Private by design** — all inference is local; nothing is sent to the cloud.\n- 🎛️ **Tunable, not magic** — weights, reliabilities, and thresholds live in an\n  editable `weights.yaml`.\n- 🖥️ **Desktop app** — Grammarly-style UI with a risk banner, attribute bar chart,\n  inline keyword highlighting, and expandable evidence; dock/float window modes.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:3","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > How it works","text":"PhishingNet > How it works\n\n[code block omitted]\n\n1. **Data prep** ([`data_prep.py`](phishing_analyzer/data_prep.py)) — merges 6\n   public corpora (Enron, CEAS_08, Nazario, Nigerian_Fraud, SpamAssassin, Ling),\n   cleans while **preserving casing/punctuation**, dedups, and makes a stratified\n   train/val/test split. See [DATA_CARD.md](DATA_CARD.md).\n2. **Feature schema** ([`lexicons.py`](phishing_analyzer/lexicons.py)) — lexicons\n   were **validated by frequency-lift against real phishing samples**, not guessed.\n3. **Extraction pipeline** ([`attributes/`](phishing_analyzer/attributes/)) — each\n   attribute is an independent, testable module returning\n   `{score, label, explanation, evidence_spans}`.\n4. **Classifier** ([`classifier.py`](phishing_analyzer/classifier.py)) — TF-IDF\n   (word 1–2 grams + char 3–5 grams) → Logistic Regression, with an exact\n   per-keyword attribution (`explain()`).\n5. **Risk combination** ([`risk.py`](phishing_analyzer/risk.py)) — noisy-OR of all\n   signals; config in [`weights.yaml`](phishing_analyzer/weights.yaml).","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:4","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > The 12 attributes","text":"PhishingNet > The 12 attributes\n\n| Attribute | Method | Detects |\n|---|---|---|\n| urgency | lexicon | time pressure (\"act now\", \"within 24 hours\") |\n| fear_threat | lexicon | intimidation (\"suspended\", \"unusual activity\") |\n| reward | lexicon | greed bait (\"you won\", \"beneficiary\", \"million dollars\") |\n| curiosity | lexicon | curiosity lures (\"you have a new message\") |\n| authority | lexicon | impersonation (\"bank\", \"security team\", brands) |\n| financial | lexicon + regex | money requests (\"wire transfer\", IBAN/amounts) |\n| credential | lexicon | harvesting phrases (\"verify your account\") |\n| generic_greeting | lexicon | \"Dear Customer/Sir/Friend\" vs personalized |\n| sender_domain | header parse | display-name vs domain spoof, lookalike domains |\n| links | URL regex | shorteners, bad TLDs, IP-literal, obfuscation |\n| grammar | spellcheck | spelling/grammar anomalies *(weak on this corpus)* |\n| caps_tone | ratios | shouting / punctuation bursts *(weak on this corpus)* |\n\nPlus the **content classifier** as a co-equal ML signal.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:5","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Risk score (noisy-OR)","text":"PhishingNet > Risk score (noisy-OR)\n\nEach signal casts a vote = `reliability × score`, then\n`risk = 1 − Π(1 − vote)` — so **any one confident signal drives risk high**, while\nsilent signals don't drag it down. A capped attribute reliability means a single\nrule tops out at \"suspicious\"; only the classifier (or several attributes) reaches\n\"phishing.\" Verdict bands: `<0.35 legitimate`, `≥0.60 phishing`, else `suspicious`.\nAll values are editable in [`weights.yaml`](phishing_analyzer/weights.yaml).","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:6","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > In-distribution (held-out test set)","text":"PhishingNet > In-distribution (held-out test set)\n\nTest set: 10,122 emails (5,824 legit / 4,298 phishing), same sources as training.\n\n| Model | Precision | Recall | F1 | ROC-AUC |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Classifier alone | 0.9916 | 0.9907 | **0.9912** | 0.9995 |\n| Combined tool | 0.9724 | 0.9919 | **0.9820** | 0.9989 |\n\nMeasured on a 3,000-email stratified sample of that test set, the real-world guards\n(classifier gate + corroboration, see below) move the **combined** tool from\nP 0.9930 / R 0.9930 / F1 0.9930 to **P 0.9922 / R 0.9844 / F1 0.9883**. Roughly half\na point of in-distribution F1 is deliberately traded away — see the next section for\nwhat it buys.\n\n> ⚠️ **These numbers do not mean the tool is 99% right on your inbox.** They are\n> in-distribution: the \"legitimate\" class is 2000s Enron internal mail, so the\n> classifier partly learns \"corporate style = safe\". While reporting F1 0.98 this\n> same build flagged a genuine Google Payments receipt as PHISHING and cleared a\n> textbook CEO gift-card scam as LEGITIMATE. A metric that cannot see a failure\n> cannot guard against it — which is what the next section is for.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:7","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Out-of-distribution (real-world corpus)","text":"PhishingNet > Out-of-distribution (real-world corpus)\n\n22 hand-built modern emails in `tests/fixtures/realworld_cases.py`: receipts, SaaS\nbilling, MFA codes, password resets, payroll, shipping, newsletters and quoted reply\nthreads on one side; classic credential lures, lookalike domains, and the\nplain-wording fraud a bag-of-words model cannot see (CEO gift-card, supplier\nbank-detail change, payroll diversion) on the other.\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| legitimate mail called **phishing** | 2 of 13 | **0 of 13** |\n| legitimate mail called suspicious | 2 of 13 | **0 of 13** |\n| fraud called **legitimate** | 1 of 9 | **0 of 9** |\n| fraud only reaching suspicious | 2 of 9 | **0 of 9** |\n| exact verdict | 15 / 22 | **22 / 22** |\n\nThe seven cases that moved:\n\n[code block omitted]\n\nThe bottom three are the \"scam worded like a normal email\" case: the TF-IDF model\nscores them 0.03-0.07, i.e. confidently *legitimate*, because there is no phishing\nvocabulary in them. They are caught by what the email asks for and who it comes\nfrom, not by what it says.\n\n```bash\n.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m evaluate_realworld     # exits non-zero on a regression\n```\n\nFull evaluation, per-source leakage probe, and per-attribute validation:","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:8","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Project structure","text":"PhishingNet > Project structure\n\n[code block omitted]\n\n> Data (`data/`), the trained model (`models/`), reports, and `node_modules/` are\n> **gitignored** — they're large and regenerable from the code (see below).\n\nRequires **Python 3.11+** (developed on 3.14) and, for the desktop app, **Node 18+**.\n\n[code block omitted]","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:9","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Usage","text":"PhishingNet > Usage\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\n.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m phishing_analyzer.cli --demo\n.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m phishing_analyzer.cli --file email.txt --json\n```\n\n**Local API server:**\n```bash\n.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m uvicorn server.app:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8008\n# GET  http://127.0.0.1:8008/health\n# POST http://127.0.0.1:8008/analyze   { \"text\": \"<raw email>\" }\n# docs http://127.0.0.1:8008/docs\n```\n\n**Desktop app** (needs the server running — see [desktop/README.md](desktop/README.md)):\n```bash\ncd desktop\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n**Tests:**\n```bash\n.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest -q\n```","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:10","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Output schema","text":"PhishingNet > Output schema\n\nEvery analysis returns a validated `AnalysisReport`\n\n[code block omitted]\n\nFull field reference: [OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md](OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md).\n\n- **Corporate-only legit baseline** — Enron is ~2000s corporate email; the model\n  can ride \"corporate style = safe.\" Per-source metrics in the evaluation expose it.\n- **Old corpus** — predates gift-card/crypto/brand-impersonation scams, so those\n  cues are retained but flagged as unvalidated on this data.\n- **grammar & caps_tone are weak here** — grammar flags corporate jargon as\n  \"misspellings\"; caps_tone doesn't separate on this corpus. Both kept at low\n  reliability and documented.\n- **No ground truth for individual attributes** — validated indirectly (AUC/\n  correlation vs the phishing label + manual spot-check).\n- **Requires the local server running** for the desktop app / API.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:11","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Real-world evaluation","text":"PhishingNet > Real-world evaluation\n\nThe test-set numbers above are in-distribution, and in-distribution metrics hid two\nreal failures: genuine receipts were called PHISHING, and a textbook CEO gift-card\nscam came back LEGITIMATE. `tests/fixtures/realworld_cases.py` is a hand-built\ncorpus of modern mail — receipts, SaaS billing, MFA codes, payroll, shipping, and\nthe plain-wording fraud a bag-of-words model cannot see — that guards both:\n\n```bash\n.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m evaluate_realworld     # exits non-zero on a regression\n```\n\nThe fixes behind it live in `phishing_analyzer/lexicons.py` (the `AMBIENT` tier),\n`weights.yaml` (`classifier_gate`, `corroboration`, `mechanism_reliability_ceiling`)\nand `attributes/bec.py`. Each carries the measurement that set it.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:12","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Team pilot mode","text":"PhishingNet > Team pilot mode\n\nThe hosted service can record a privacy-bounded row per analysis so a small team can\nmeasure the tool on real mail and feed corrections back in — verdicts, signals and\nsender *domains* only; never a subject, body, link or address. It ships with a\ndashboard (`/dashboard`), a JSON API (`/stats`, `/export`), an in-banner correction\nbutton, and a privacy audit that fails the build if message content ever reaches the\ndatabase. It is off unless explicitly enabled at both ends.\n\n**See [PILOT.md](PILOT.md)** for the full rollout: what to measure, the consent\nconversation, the week-by-week runbook, and what the data cannot tell you.","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:github_repo:13","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/TIS","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"PhishingNet > Roadmap","text":"PhishingNet > Roadmap\n\n- Modern/held-out phishing evaluation to quantify real-world generalization.\n- Optional bundling of the Python backend with the desktop app.\n\n| Doc | What's in it |\n|---|---|\n| [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md) | Project context, decisions, and gotchas (authoritative) |\n| [DATA_CARD.md](DATA_CARD.md) | Dataset sources, sizes, splits, biases |\n| [FEATURE_SCHEMA.md](FEATURE_SCHEMA.md) | The 12 attributes + validation findings |\n| [OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md](OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md) | JSON output contract + CLI usage |\n| [PILOT.md](PILOT.md) | Running a small-team pilot: setup, consent, runbook, caveats |\n| [desktop/README.md](desktop/README.md) | Desktop app run instructions |\n\n---\n\n*Built as an educational, defensive security tool. Detection quality is\ndemonstrated on a public research corpus; validate before relying on it for\nreal-world protection.*","vector":null},{"id":"phishingnet:live_demo:0","projectId":"phishingnet","title":"PhishingNet","sourceUrl":"https://dubaiplayer.github.io/TIS/","sourceType":"live_demo","heading":"PhishingNet","text":"PhishingNet\n\nPhishingNet PhishingNet: privacy policy and project documentation View the Project on GitHub dubaiplayer/TIS TIS — Phishing Email Analyzer “Grammarly for phishing.” Paste or open an email and get an explainable, structured breakdown of its manipulation / phishing tactics — each attribute scored, with the exact triggering evidence highlighted — plus one overall risk verdict. Interpretability first: every score traces back to evidence, not a black box, and everything runs 100% locally (no email leaves your machine). Table of contents What it is Key features How it works The 12 attributes Risk score (noisy-OR) Results Project structure Setup Usage Output schema Known limitations & biases Roadmap Documentation What it is TIS reads a raw email (headers + body, or just body) and returns: An overall risk verdict — phishing / suspicious / legitimate with a 0–1 score. 12 per-attribute scores — urgency, fear/threat, reward bait, authority impersonation, financial requests, credential harvesting, generic greetings, sender-domain spoofing, suspicious links, grammar, caps/tone — each with a plain-English explanation and character-offset evidence spans for highlighting. Classifier keyword attributions — the specific words/n-grams that drove the ML model’s prediction (exact coef × tf-idf contributions), with intensities. It ships three front-ends over one Python engine: a CLI, a local FastAPI server, and a desktop app (Electron + React). Key features 🔍 Explainable — per-attribute evidence spans + per-keyword ML attributions. 🧠 Hybrid detection — 10 rule/lexicon attributes + 2 heuristics + a TF-IDF/ Logistic-Regression classifier, fused by a transparent, tunable noisy-OR. 🔒 Private by design — all inference is local; nothing is sent to the cloud. 🎛️ Tunable, not magic — weights, reliabilities, and thresholds live in an editable weights.yaml. 🖥️ Desktop app — Grammarly-style UI with a risk banner, attribute bar chart, inline keyword highlighting, and expandable evidence; dock/float window modes. How it works raw email ──► text_clean ──► ┌───────────────────────────────┐ (parse │ 12 attribute scorers (rules) │──┐ headers, └───────────────────────────────┘ │ noisy-OR clean body, ┌───────────────────────────────┐ ├──► risk_score keep casing) │ TF-IDF + LogReg classifier │──┘ + verdict │ (probability + keyword attr.) │ └───────────────────────────────┘ Data prep (data_prep.py) — merges 6 public corpora (Enron, CEAS_08, Nazario, Nigerian_Fraud, SpamAssassin, Ling), cleans while preserving casing/punctuation, dedups, and makes a stratified train/val/test split. See DATA_CARD.md. Feature schema (lexicons.py) — lexicons were validated by frequency-lift against real phishing samples, not guessed. See FEATURE_SCHEMA.md. Extraction pipeline (attributes/) — each attribute is an independent, testable module returning {score, label, explanation, evidence_spans}. Classifier (classifier.py) — TF-IDF (word 1–2 grams + char 3–5 grams) → Logistic Regression, with an exact per-keyword attribution (explain()). Risk combination (risk.py) — noisy-OR of all signals; config in weights.yaml. The 12 attributes Attribute Method Detects urgency lexicon time pressure (“act now”, “within 24 hours”) fear_threat lexicon intimidation (“suspended”, “unusual activity”) reward lexicon greed bait (“you won”, “beneficiary”, “million dollars”) curiosity lexicon curiosity lures (“you have a new message”) authority lexicon impersonation (“bank”, “security team”, brands) financial lexicon + regex money requests (“wire transfer”, IBAN/amounts) credential lexicon harvesting phrases (“verify your account”) generic_greeting lexicon “Dear Customer/Sir/Friend” vs personalized sender_domain header parse display-name vs domain spoof, lookalike domains links URL regex shorteners, bad TLDs, IP-literal, obfuscation grammar spellcheck spelling/grammar anomalies (weak on this corpus) caps_tone ratios shouting / punctuation bursts (weak on this corpus) Plus the content classifier as a co-equal ML signal. Risk score (noisy-OR) Each signal casts a vote = reliability × score, then risk = 1 − Π(1 − vote) — so any one confident signal drives risk high, while silent signals don’t drag it down. A capped attribute reliability means a single rule tops out at “suspicious”; only the classifier (or several attributes) reaches “phishing.” Verdict bands: <0.35 legitimate, ≥0.60 phishing, else suspicious. All values are editable in weights.yaml. Results In-distribution (held-out test set) Test set: 10,122 emails (5,824 legit / 4,298 phishing), same sources as training. Model Precision Recall F1 ROC-AUC Classifier alone 0.9916 0.9907 0.9912 0.9995 Combined tool 0.9724 0.9919 0.9820 0.9989 Measured on a 3,000-email stratified sample of that test set, the real-world guards (classifier gate + corroboration, see below) move the combined tool from P 0.9930 / R 0.9930 / F1 0.9930 to P 0.9922 / R 0.9844 / F1 0.9883. Roughly half a point of in-distribution F1 is deliberately traded away — see the next section for what it buys. ⚠️ These numbers do not mean the tool is 99% right on your inbox. They are in-distribution: the “legitimate” class is 2000s Enron internal mail, so the classifier partly learns “corporate style = safe”. While reporting F1 0.98 this same build flagged a genuine Google Payments receipt as PHISHING and cleared a textbook CEO gift-card scam as LEGITIMATE. A metric that cannot see a failure cannot guard against it — which is what the next section is for. Out-of-distribution (real-world corpus) 22 hand-built modern emails in tests/fixtures/realworld_cases.py: receipts, SaaS billing, MFA codes, password resets, payroll, shipping, newsletters and quoted reply threads on one side; classic credential lures, lookalike domains, and the plain-wording fraud a bag-of-words model cannot see (CEO gift-card, supplier bank-detail change, payroll diversion) on the other. before after legitimate mail called phishing 2 of 13 0 of 13 legitimate mail called suspicious 2 of 13 0 of 13 fraud called legitimate 1 of 9 0 of 9 fraud only reaching suspicious 2 of 9 0 of 9 exact verdict 15 / 22 22 / 22 The seven cases that moved: google_payments_receipt phishing 0.76 -> legitimate 0.13 atlassian_subscription phishing 0.66 -> legitimate 0.00 internal_invoice_approval suspicious 0.40 -> legitimate 0.00 it_maintenance_notice suspicious 0.42 -> legitimate 0.00 bec_ceo_giftcard legitimate 0.07 -> phishing 0.64 <- classifier says 0.07 payroll_diversion suspicious 0.48 -> phishing 0.72 <- classifier says 0.03 ceo_wire_urgent suspicious 0.40 -> phishing 0.70 <- classifier says 0.06 The bottom three are the “scam worded like a normal email” case: the TF-IDF model scores them 0.03-0.07, i.e. confidently legitimate, because there is no phishing vocabulary in them. They are caught by what the email asks for and who it comes from, not by what it says. .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m evaluate_realworld # exits non-zero on a regression Full evaluation, per-source leakage probe, and per-attribute validation: evaluate.py. Project structure TIS/ ├── phishing_analyzer/ # core engine │ ├── data_prep.py # merge/clean/split the corpora │ ├── text_clean.py # casing-preserving cleaner │ ├── lexicons.py # data-validated keyword lists │ ├── attributes/ # 12 independent attribute modules │ ├── classifier.py # TF-IDF + LogReg (+ keyword attribution) │ ├── risk.py + weights.yaml # noisy-OR risk combination (tunable) │ ├── schema.py # pydantic AnalysisReport (JSON contract) │ ├── pipeline.py # orchestrator: raw email -> report │ └── cli.py # terminal demo with inline highlighting ├── server/app.py # FastAPI local API (/analyze, /health) ├── desktop/ # Electron + React desktop app ├── evaluate.py # test-set metrics + validation ├── scripts/ # data build + lexicon validation utilities ├── tests/ # pytest (attribute unit tests) ├── DATA_CARD.md / FEATURE_SCHEMA.md / OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md / CLAUDE.md └── requirements.txt Data (data/), the trained model (models/), reports, and node_modules/ are gitignored — they’re large and regenerable from the code (see below). Setup Requires Python 3.11+ (developed on 3.14) and, for the desktop app, Node 18+. # from TIS/ python -m venv .venv .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.txt # Windows # (macOS/Linux: .venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements.txt) # build the processed data + train the model (one time; regenerates gitignored artifacts) .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m scripts.build_enron_raw # samples raw Enron .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m phishing_analyzer.data_prep # -> data/processed/ .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m phishing_analyzer.classifier # -> models/classifier.joblib Usage CLI: .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m phishing_analyzer.cli --demo .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m phishing_analyzer.cli --file email.txt --json Local API server: .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m uvicorn server.app:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8008 # GET http://127.0.0.1:8008/health # POST http://127.0.0.1:8008/analyze { \"text\": \"<raw email>\" } # docs http://127.0.0.1:8008/docs Desktop app (needs the server running — see desktop/README.md): cd desktop npm install npm run dev Tests: .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest -q Output schema Every analysis returns a validated AnalysisReport (schema.py): { \"verdict\": \"phishing | suspicious | legitimate\", \"risk_score\": 0.0, \"summary\": \"One-line explanation with top signals\", \"top_signals\": [\"content_classifier\", \"financial\", \"sender_domain\"], \"attributes\": [ { \"name\": \"financial\", \"score\": 0.98, \"label\": \"high financial\", \"explanation\": \"matched 6 financial cue(s): ...\", \"evidence\": [{ \"text\": \"wire transfer\", \"start\": 42, \"end\": 55 }] } ], \"classifier\": { \"phishing_probability\": 0.9996, \"keyword_attributions\": [ { \"term\": \"verify your account\", \"weight\": 0.38, \"direction\": \"phishing\", \"intensity\": 1.0, \"spans\": [/* ... */] } ], \"char_ngram_contribution\": 4.64 }, \"meta\": { \"sender_analyzed\": true, \"notes\": [] }, \"analyzed_text\": \"Subject...\\n\\nbody...\" } Full field reference: OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md. Known limitations & biases Corporate-only legit baseline — Enron is ~2000s corporate email; the model can ride “corporate style = safe.” Per-source metrics in the evaluation expose it. Old corpus — predates gift-card/crypto/brand-impersonation scams, so those cues are retained but flagged as unvalidated on this data. grammar & caps_tone are weak here — grammar flags corporate jargon as “misspellings”; caps_tone doesn’t separate on this corpus. Both kept at low reliability and documented. No ground truth for individual attributes — validated indirectly (AUC/ correlation vs the phishing label + manual spot-check). Requires the local server running for the desktop app / API. Real-world evaluation The test-set numbers above are in-distribution, and in-distribution metrics hid two real failures: genuine receipts were called PHISHING, and a textbook CEO gift-card scam came back LEGITIMATE. tests/fixtures/realworld_cases.py is a hand-built corpus of modern mail — receipts, SaaS billing, MFA codes, payroll, shipping, and the plain-wording fraud a bag-of-words model cannot see — that guards both: .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m evaluate_realworld # exits non-zero on a regression The fixes behind it live in phishing_analyzer/lexicons.py (the AMBIENT tier), weights.yaml (classifier_gate, corroboration, mechanism_reliability_ceiling) and attributes/bec.py. Each carries the measurement that set it. Team pilot mode The hosted service can record a privacy-bounded row per analysis so a small team can measure the tool on real mail and feed corrections back in — verdicts, signals and sender domains only; never a subject, body, link or address. It ships with a dashboard (/dashboard), a JSON API (/stats, /export), an in-banner correction button, and a privacy audit that fails the build if message content ever reaches the database. It is off unless explicitly enabled at both ends. See PILOT.md for the full rollout: what to measure, the consent conversation, the week-by-week runbook, and what the data cannot tell you. Roadmap Modern/held-out phishing evaluation to quantify real-world generalization. Optional bundling of the Python backend with the desktop app. Documentation Doc What’s in it CLAUDE.md Project context, decisions, and gotchas (authoritative) DATA_CARD.md Dataset sources, sizes, splits, biases FEATURE_SCHEMA.md The 12 attributes + validation findings OUTPUT_SCHEMA.md JSON output contract + CLI usage PILOT.md Running a small-team pilot: setup, consent, runbook, caveats desktop/README.md Desktop app run instructions Built as an educational, defensive security tool. Detection quality is demonstrated on a public research corpus; validate before relying on it for real-world protection. This project is maintained by dubaiplayer Hosted on GitHub Pages — Theme by orderedlist","vector":null},{"id":"portfolio-site:github_repo:0","projectId":"portfolio-site","title":"Portfolio Site","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/portfolio","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"Portfolio Site","text":"Portfolio Site\n\nRepository description: My portfolio website to find info on me.\nLanguages: JavaScript, CSS, HTML\nHomepage: https://deveshgupta.vercel.app","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:0","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock","text":"AgentDock\n\nLanguages: HTML, JavaScript, Shell, PowerShell\n\nA task-delegation bulletin board for AI agents. Any agent can **post** a task it needs\nhelp with; other agents can **discover** open tasks, **claim** one, and **post a result**\nback — a shared, stateless place for agents to hand work to each other asynchronously.\n\n- **Stack:** Node.js + Express, with SQLite via Node's built-in `node:sqlite` module (no\n  external database, no native build step).\n- **Auth:** none (MVP — friction is deliberately zero).\n- **Agent-facing docs:** [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md), also served live at `GET /skill.md`.\n- **Human dashboard (for demos):** open the base URL (`GET /`) in a browser for a live,\n  auto-refreshing board showing tasks move **open → claimed → done**. It's a read-only\n  viewer over the same public API — agents never need it. See\n\n> **Why `node:sqlite` instead of `better-sqlite3`?** `node:sqlite` is built into Node, so\n> `npm install` pulls only Express and there is **no native compilation** to fail on any\n> machine or host. Same SQLite storage, zero extra friction.","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:1","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > Requirements","text":"AgentDock > Requirements\n\n- **Node.js 24** (for the built-in `node:sqlite` module, flag-free). Check with `node -v`.\n\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm start\n```\n\nThe server listens on `http://localhost:3000` (or `$PORT` if set). Data is stored in a\nlocal `agentdock.db` file. To use a throwaway in-memory database instead:\n\n```bash\nDB_PATH=:memory: npm start\n```","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:2","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > Test the full lifecycle","text":"AgentDock > Test the full lifecycle\n\nWith the server running in one terminal, in another:\n\n```bash\n./test.sh\n# or point it at any deployment:\nBASE_URL=https://your-app.up.railway.app ./test.sh\n```\n\n`test.sh` exercises create → list → claim → complete → get, plus the 409/403/404/400\nerror paths, and exits non-zero if anything is off. Only `curl` is required (`jq` is used\nfor pretty-printing if present).","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:3","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > API summary","text":"AgentDock > API summary\n\n| Method & path              | Purpose                                  |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| `GET  /health`             | Uptime check → `{\"status\":\"ok\"}`         |\n| `GET  /skill.md`           | The SKILL.md doc as raw `text/markdown`  |\n| `POST /tasks`              | Create a task                            |\n| `GET  /tasks`              | List tasks (`?status=open\\|claimed\\|done`) |\n| `GET  /tasks/:id`          | Get one task                             |\n| `POST /tasks/:id/claim`    | Claim an open task                       |\n| `POST /tasks/:id/complete` | Submit a result for a claimed task       |\n\nFull request/response examples are in [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md).","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:4","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > Environment variables","text":"AgentDock > Environment variables\n\n| Variable  | Default              | Notes                                                    |\n|-----------|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\n| `PORT`    | `3000`               | Set automatically by Railway/Render — no action needed.  |\n| `DB_PATH` | `./agentdock.db`     | SQLite file path. Use `:memory:` for an ephemeral DB.    |","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:5","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > Deploy","text":"AgentDock > Deploy\n\nThis repo runs with **zero config** beyond `npm install && npm start`. Both hosts below\ndetect Node automatically, inject `PORT`, and need no environment variables.\n\n> Ensure the host uses **Node 24** (the `.nvmrc`, the `render.yaml` blueprint, and the\n> `engines` field all point there). Node 24 runs the built-in `node:sqlite` module with\n> no experimental flag; older 22.x lines can require one, so stick with 24.","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:6","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > Railway (CLI, one command after login)","text":"AgentDock > Railway (CLI, one command after login)\n\n```bash\nnpm i -g @railway/cli\nrailway login\nrailway init          # create a project (accept defaults)\nrailway up            # build & deploy from this directory\nrailway domain        # generate a public https URL\n```\n\nOr via the dashboard: **New Project → Deploy from GitHub repo → select this repo.**\nRailway runs `npm install` then `npm start` automatically.","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:7","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > Render (dashboard)","text":"AgentDock > Render (dashboard)\n\nThis repo includes a `render.yaml` blueprint, so the fastest path is:\n\n1. Push this repo to GitHub.\n2. In Render: **New → Blueprint → connect the repo.** Render reads `render.yaml` and\n   pre-fills the build/start commands and `NODE_VERSION=24` — just click **Apply**.\n3. Render provides an `https://…onrender.com` URL.\n\nPrefer to configure by hand? Use **New → Web Service** instead, with Runtime **Node**,\nBuild Command `npm install`, Start Command `npm start`, and env var `NODE_VERSION=24`.","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:8","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > After deploying","text":"AgentDock > After deploying\n\nOnce you have your public URL (e.g. `https://agentdock-production.up.railway.app`):\n\n1. Confirm it's live: `curl https://<your-url>/health` → `{\"status\":\"ok\"}`.\n2. **Edit [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md):** replace every `https://YOUR-DEPLOY-URL` with your\n   real URL, then redeploy so `GET /skill.md` serves the corrected doc.\n3. Smoke-test end-to-end: `BASE_URL=https://<your-url> ./test.sh`.","vector":null},{"id":"agentdock:github_repo:9","projectId":"agentdock","title":"AgentDock","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/NANDA","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"AgentDock > Notes","text":"AgentDock > Notes\n\n- SQLite is stored on the instance's local disk. On hosts with an ephemeral filesystem\n  (Render free tier, Railway without a volume) the DB resets on redeploy/restart — fine\n  for a hackathon demo. Attach a persistent volume if you need durability.\n- No auth, no rate limiting by design. Do not put anything sensitive in a task.","vector":null},{"id":"lyricmatcher:github_repo:0","projectId":"lyricmatcher","title":"Resonate","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/dubaiplayer/lyricmatcher","sourceType":"github_repo","heading":"Resonate","text":"Resonate\n\nRepository description: Recommends songs based on lyrical content based on emotion.\nLanguages: Python, TypeScript, CSS, JavaScript, HTML\nHomepage: https://lyricmatcher.vercel.app","vector":null}]};
